                <pb facs="0973" n="859" sameAs="frus:frus1931v01/pg859"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">711.632/79: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Minister in Austria</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Stockton</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of
                    State</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Vienna</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1930-12-11T17:00:00+01:00">December 11, 1930—5
                        p.m.</date><lb/>[Received 6:46 p.m.]</dateline></opener>
                <p>64. Department’s telegram No. 45, December 8, 6 p.m. Had satisfactory conference
                    with Schüller who has agreed in principle and to methods proposed. He assures me
                    of his cooperation and will push matter energetically. Cannot urge too strongly
                    that way be paved to secure Senate’s ratification, during present session, of
                    supplementary article as soon as Austrian Government has ratified it and
                    approved proposed exchange of notes.</p>
                <p>[Paraphrase.] In view of the economic conditions now prevailing in Central and
                    Southeastern Europe, which have resulted in increasing opposition here to
                    most-favored-nation clause, I consider treaty more important to us than to
                    Austria and feel that it is extremely advisable to effect exchange of
                    ratifications at the earliest possible date. Progress of negotiations will be
                    reported to Department by telegraph. [End paraphrase.]</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Stockton</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
